  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nLake effect rain showers will mix with snow at times inland and\naway from Lake Michigan early this morning, as the overall band\nof precipitation slowly shifts east into northwest IN this \nmorning and then east of the area this afternoon. Not expecting \nmuch in the way of snow accumulation, but along the edges of \nthis band of precipitation, where temperatures have cooled into \nthe lower 30s, some minor snow accumulation on grassy or \nelevated surfaces remains possible overnight. Once this \nprecipitation ends later this afternoon, dry weather is expected\nthrough at least next Wednesday.\n\nWhile overnight low temperatures will be below normal for mid\nNovember, in the 20s away from Chicago/Lake Michigan, daytime\nhighs will rebound to near normal by Sunday and then likely\nabove normal every day next week with highs well into the 50s\nand perhaps pushing 60 by the end of next week. Low temps will \neventually warm as well and by mid/late next week, possibly be \nin the 40s.\n\nOnly other weather item of concern to watch for is the potential\nfor fog the next several nights. Light winds at the surface and\nlight winds aloft with limited/no mixing may allow for some\nshallow/patchy fog to develop Saturday night and again Sunday\nnight. Confidence is too low for any fog mention for now. cms",
